{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "chair_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_chair_summary_v1.md",
  "summary": {
    "leading_positions": [
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B003"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "johansson-goran"
        ],
        "synthesis": "Circuit QED is presented as a mature platform that established controllable light–matter interactions in superconducting circuits. Its proponents distinguish that foundational advance from later processor engineering; the boundary with earlier circuit work and the 2025 prize, and attribution within the wider field, remain open."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B016"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "danielsson-ulf"
        ],
        "synthesis": "WMAP is credited with making cosmology quantitative through precise CMB mapping. The proposed account joins mission leadership, instrumentation, and interpretation, while the mission's relationship to earlier and later CMB work and individual credit within a large collaboration need review."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B010"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "lindroth-eva"
        ],
        "synthesis": "The recollision model and its quantum theory are proposed as a bounded, fundamental account of laser-driven electron motion. The distinction from the 2023 attosecond-pulse prize and later experimental development needs clarification; attribution focuses on Lewenstein and Corkum within a wider field."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B054"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "olsson-eva"
        ],
        "synthesis": "Aberration correction is presented as an instrument advance that enabled atomic-resolution imaging and has established impact. The central unresolved issue is how to distinguish Rose's optical theory from the corrector development and experimental realization associated with Haider, Urban, and others."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B014"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "eriksson-olle"
        ],
        "synthesis": "Room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance is proposed for its mature physical and technological impact. The citation must distinguish it from the giant magnetoresistance work recognized in 2007, and the chronology and respective contributions of the three proposed laureates require review."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B011"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "kroll-stefan"
        ],
        "synthesis": "Squeezed light is proposed as a demonstrated method of quantum-noise reduction in interferometry. The account links theory, optical development, and detector use, while the roles of Schnabel and broader teams and the boundary with gravitational-wave interferometry need review."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B026"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "mehlig-bernhard"
        ],
        "synthesis": "The KPZ framework and experimental confirmation are presented as a compact, mature achievement with reach across statistical physics. The central question is whether the proposed three names fairly represent the distinct theoretical and experimental contributions."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B061"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "pearce-mark"
        ],
        "synthesis": "The Higgs discovery is presented as a mature result independently established by ATLAS and CMS. Its discovery claim is distinct from the earlier theoretical prediction, but selecting a few experimental leaders from two large collaborations requires careful contribution review."
      }
    ],
    "areas_of_agreement": [
      "Several accounts treat attribution in large collaborations or instrument efforts as a substantive issue: WMAP, the Higgs discovery, aberration-corrected microscopy, and squeezed-light interferometry each require a defensible explanation of individual contributions.",
      "Responses to the circuit-QED proposal recognize its scientific strength and mature experimental platform, while treating its boundary with prior superconducting-circuit work and the 2025 prize as a separate question."
    ],
    "scientific_disputes": [
      "The scope of circuit QED remains contested: the proposed case centers on controllable photon–artificial-atom interactions, while the committee must determine how clearly that advance can be separated from earlier circuit physics and subsequent quantum-computing engineering.",
      "The relationship of electron recollision to the 2023 attosecond-pulse prize needs clarification: the proposal distinguishes a physical mechanism from pulse-generation capability, but the overlap in scientific scope remains unresolved.",
      "The WMAP case must define its distinctive contribution relative to earlier CMB work and later, more precise observations."
    ],
    "attribution_disputes": [
      "The committee needs historical review of individual credit in WMAP, ATLAS and CMS, aberration-corrected microscopy, and the development and deployment of squeezed light.",
      "The proposed attribution for room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance needs a supported chronology for Moodera, Parkin, and Miyazaki, alongside a clear distinction from the 2007 GMR award.",
      "The relative credit for the KPZ theory and its experimental confirmation, and for the theory and experiments underlying circuit QED, remains to be established."
    ],
    "maturity_disputes": [
      "Circuit QED's core platform is described as mature, while the ultimate impact of fault-tolerant quantum computing remains unsettled; the committee must decide how much the latter bears on the case for the former.",
      "The proposals generally describe established results or capabilities, but the committee has not compared how their demonstrated longevity and independent validation should weigh against future technological impact."
    ],
    "conflict_or_recusal_flags": [
      "Johansson reports directing WACQT and working in superconducting circuits; he identifies no direct collaboration or institutional tie to the proposed circuit-QED laureates, but the field connection warrants review.",
      "Lindroth reports long-standing collaboration with Anne L'Huillier and coauthorship with members of the Lund attosecond group. They are not named in B010, but the connection to the research area may merit recusal review.",
      "Pearce reports training in experimental particle physics at CERN and flags this as a potential field connection to B061; his profile identifies no work with ATLAS or CMS or with the proposed laureates."
    ],
    "questions_for_round2": [
      "For circuit QED, what historical and scientific boundary separates the proposed photon–artificial-atom advance from earlier superconducting-circuit work and the 2025 prize, and which contributions support the proposed three-name attribution?",
      "For electron recollision, how does the scope of the 2023 attosecond-pulse prize bear on a citation centered on the recollision mechanism and its quantum theory?",
      "What evidence supports the proposed individual credit for WMAP, ATLAS and CMS, aberration-corrected microscopy, and squeezed-light interferometry, and how should the citation acknowledge the wider teams?",
      "For room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance, what chronology and attribution distinguish the proposed achievement from the 2007 giant magnetoresistance award?",
      "Do the reported research-area connections for Johansson, Lindroth, and Pearce require any recusal or other process action?"
    ],
    "chair_observation": "The opening positions identify several distinct, mature achievements, but they differ in scientific scope and in how readily individual contributions can be bounded. Round 2 should test those boundaries and the supporting attribution evidence without treating ranking or nomination frequency as a substitute for explicit preference."
  }
}
